00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:03.760
Well, he and Beaver are such good friends...

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:07.150
that I think this is the first place he'd come to.

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.540
Well, that's possible, but so far he hasn't shown up.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:23.320
Well, Mrs. Mondello, I told Beaver if he saw Larry around anywhere to be sure and let me know immediately.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.760
And we'll certainly keep our eyes open.

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.690
Well, thank you, Mr. Cleaver.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:28.890
Thank you so much.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.460
Cleaver, that's Larry's suitcase.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.020
It's going up in the air.

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:45.200
Yes, it certainly is, Mrs. Mondello.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:45.980
Excuse me a minute.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:49.280
June? June?

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.710
Yes, dear? What's the matter?

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.010
Mrs. Mondello's in here. I think we found Larry.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:55.880
Where?

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:56.980
I'll let you know in a minute.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:10.930
Ah.

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:11.630
Hi, Dad.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:13.150
Where is he?

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:14.490
Huh?

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:15.890
Never mind.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:48.570
Hello, beaver.

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:50.050
Where is he?

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:52.480
Who did?

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:54.980
fellows.

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:56.230
Come in here a minute.

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:04.450
You know, fellas, uh...

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:06.120
I've been thinking about you.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:08.840
You've had a long, hard day today.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:10.900
You know what would make you feel just great?

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.130
A nice hot shower.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:14.350
Thank you.

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:18.060
Ah.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:19.320
A shower, Dad?

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:19.960
Yeah.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:21.570
I tell you what.

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.220
You fellas go ahead and get ready, and I'll turn the water on.

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:25.860
Get it good and steaming hot.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:30.920
All right.

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:32.380
You can come on out now.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:35.320
Thank you.

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:37.540
Hello, Mr. Cleaver.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:38.830
Hello, Larry.

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:40.370
Well.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.990
Wally and Beaver didn't tell me they had a guest this evening.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:44.730
No.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:46.940
No, I guess not.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:50.500
There's someone downstairs who's very anxious to see you.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.300
Is my father home from Cincinnati?

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:54.570
But your mother's here.

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:56.700
I think you better come on downstairs right now.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:19:59.540
Wally, you got the suitcase.

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:09.120
Do you want us to come downstairs, too, Dad?

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.550
No. I want you to stay right here.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:16.030
And while you're up here, I want you both to think of all the deceitful things you've done today.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.660
Behind my back and behind your mother's back.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:19.980
Yes, sir.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:20.780
Yes, sir.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:23.260
Mr. Cleaver?

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:23.660
Yes?

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.760
Is my sister here, too?

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:27.540
She's waiting in the car.

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:29.210
That's good.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.170
Because I don't want you to see me get it.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:40.040
Boy, and he is going to get it, too.

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:48.020
Yeah, and after he gets it, we're going to get it.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:54.520
And another thing, Larry, you're not going to the movies

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:56.880
or watch television for a whole month.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:58.490
Yes, Mom.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.620
I just can't understand how you could be such a bad, bad boy

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:08.100
and how you could do a thing like this to your poor mother who loves you.

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:12.160
I don't see what possible reason you could have had for running away.

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:16.480
If I told you, you wouldn't even listen.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:18.740
Why, of course your mother would, Larry.

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:23.510
I don't mind you telling me I'm a bad, dumb kid when I'm just by myself,

00:21:23.530 --> 00:21:27.910
but you told me I was a bad, dumb kid in front of Beaver.

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:29.980
Now you're telling me I'm...

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:36.100
a bad dumb kid in front of the whole world mr cleaver have you ever heard anything like that

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:41.150
in your whole life well i don't know if i've ever heard it but i imagine a lot of children

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:47.830
have thought it you see mom he's on my side oh now just a minute larry i can understand how

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:52.810
children don't want to be yelled at but i'm not on any boy's side when it comes to running away

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:58.310
you should realize the worry you've caused your mother and how she felt when she didn't know

00:21:58.310 --> 00:21:58.950
where you were.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.660
You'd just better hope that when you grow up,

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.480
no one ever does a thing like this to you.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:07.600
Yes, sir. I'm sorry, Mom.

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.610
Well, Larry, we'd better go now.

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.210
Your sister has to comb out her hair.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:17.590
Gee, Mr. Cleaver, I understand, kids.

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:19.670
You're a real neat father.

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:21.440
Uh, yes.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.220
Well, Larry, sometimes it's a lot easier

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:26.680
to be a real neat father to someone else's kids

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:27.560
than it is to your own.

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:29.980
Thank you so much, Mr. Cleaver.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:32.380
And thank you, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:34.460
I'm glad everything's gonna be all right.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:40.580
They sure been talking for a long time.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:43.960
Yeah, there's three of them and only one of him.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:45.100
Yeah.

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:49.100
Hey, that was the front door.

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:56.600
Here goes Larry and his mom.

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:57.880
Is he crying?

00:22:59.360 --> 00:22:59.980
Not enough so you can.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:00.960
You could hear him up here.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:05.190
Hey, they're getting in the car.

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:07.680
You can see his sister.

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.320
Is she laughing at him?

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.600
No, but she's kind of smiling.

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:23.440
Well, I guess I better go up and talk to our two now.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:24.630
I suppose so.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:28.180
But, dear, try to be a real neat father, will you,

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:29.440
even though they are our kids?

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:31.350
Keep that in mind.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:44.590
Boy, Wally, we must have pulled a million weeds already.

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:48.350
Yeah, and Dad's gonna make us pull a million more tomorrow.

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:51.070
He's being so mean to us.

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:55.110
Well, he's not being mean to us. He's just punishing us.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:59.390
But we had to hide Larry, and make believe he wasn't there.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:01.320
and kind of he's my best friend.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.450
Yeah, yeah, but you know, I've been thinking.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:06.760
We could have just gone to Dad

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:09.020
and told him that Larry was in trouble and ran away.

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:10.930
You know how Dad is.

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:13.120
He probably would have tried to work it out.

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:16.140
Sure would have been a lot easier than the way we did it.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:17.990
Sure would.

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:20.840
Stick it the easy way.

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:22.530
Well, after we already got in trouble.

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.040
Well, the thing is,

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:27.170
when you're in it, you don't know it's trouble.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:29.490
But it just turns out to be trouble

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:29.970
after you get in trouble.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.520
Hey, that's pretty good, Wally. Did you learn that in high school?

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:35.270
Nah, I just thought of it.

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:37.610
You're thinking, don't you, Wally?

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:41.730
Well, lots of times when I'm doing nothing, I'm thinking.

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.870
Well, I guess that's on account of you getting older.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:48.790
Because when I'm doing nothing, I'm redoing nothing.
